\section{Introduction}


Problem 4:

\noindent\emph{ Find the least area which can be included between two parabolas whose axes are parallel and
at a distance 2, and which cut each other at right angles in two points.} \\

\begin{figure}[h!]
     \centering
     \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{image2.png}
     \caption{Graph of the Problem}
     \label{fig:placeholder}
 \end{figure} 


A parabola is a conic section which is U-shaped where all points are equidistant from a fixed
point (the focus) and a fixed line (the directrix). After we read the problem, we firstly decided on an algebraic approach. We knew that we needed to use two equations of parabolas with unknown coefficients which are at a distance of 2 from each other. The equations we decided to use are: 
\begin{align*}
    y_1=&ax^2\\
    y_2=&b(x-2)^2+c,
\end{align*}

where $a,b,c\in\mathbb{R}$. 

\noindent This is because they contain the least number of unknowns whilst still satisfying the conditions of the problem. Also they are just simply transformations of quadratic curves so we knew that the solution to these particular equations would also work for any other transformation of quadratic curves.\\

