\subsection{Alternate Solution 1}

\textit{As is often the case in mathematics, there are many ways to solve this problem. Here we will look at a different approach to subsection 3.1; Expressing b and c in terms of a.}\\
\\We have that \textit{$l_1$} and \textit{$l_2$} intersect at
\begin{align*}
    (a-b)x^2+4bx-(4b+c)=0\\
\end{align*}
from which we can obtain an equation for $x$ using the quadratic formula
\\
\begin{align*}
    x = \frac{-4b \pm \sqrt{16b^2 + 4(a-b)(c+4b)}}{2(a-b)}.
\end{align*}
which simplifies to
\begin{align*}
    x = \frac{-2b \pm \sqrt{4b^2 + (a-b)(c+4b)}}{(a-b)}.
\end{align*}To make the following algebraic steps easier, let $\mu = 4b^2 + (a-b)(c+4b)$. We also have
\begin{align*}
    2ax\cdot2b(x-2)=-1\\
\end{align*}
at the points of intersection. This is because the lines are perpendicular, as noted in our original solution.  
Substituting both of our solutions for $x$ into this equation gives us

\begin{align}
    \nonumber\frac{-2b+\sqrt{\mu}}{a-b}\cdot\frac{-2b+\sqrt{\mu}-2(a-b)}{a-b}=-\frac{1}{4ab}
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
    \nonumber\frac{-2b-\sqrt{\mu}}{a-b}\cdot\frac{-2b-\sqrt{\mu}-2(a-b)}{a-b}=-\frac{1}{4ab}.
\end{align}
\\
We can now equate these two equations:
\\
\\
\begin{align}
    \nonumber\frac{-2b+\sqrt{\mu}}{a-b}\cdot\frac{-2b+\sqrt{\mu}-2(a-b)}{a-b}=\frac{-2b-\sqrt{\mu}}{a-b}\cdot\frac{-2b-\sqrt{\mu}-2(a-b)}{a-b}
\end{align}
\\which simplifies to
\\
\\
\begin{align*}
    \left(-2b+\sqrt{\mu}\right)\left(-2a+\sqrt{\mu}\right)=\left(-2b-\sqrt{\mu}\right)\left(-2a-\sqrt{\mu}\right).
\end{align*}
\\
This further simplifies to $a=-b$. We can now substitute this into one of the two equations obtained from the quadratic and the perpendicular property:


\[
\frac{2a\pm\sqrt{4a^2+(a+a)(c-4a)}}{a+a}
\cdot
\frac{2a\pm\sqrt{4a^2+(a+a)(c-4a)}-2(a+a)}{a+a}
=\frac{1}{4a^2} .
\]
\\ We can now solve for $c$ and obtain
\begin{align*}
    c = 4a + \frac{1}{2a}
\end{align*}

\noindent as before.
\subsection{Alternate Solution 2}
\textit{While working towards a solution, some of us used a different method when looking at subsection 3.1; Expressing b and c in terms of a. This method uses Vieta's formulas.}\\
\\We have that $l_1$ and $l_2$ intersect at 
\begin{align*}
    (a-b)x^2+4bx-(4b+c)=0\\
\end{align*}
And we also have that the tangents to $l_1$ and $l_2$ are at right angles to each other so 
\begin{align*}
    4abx^2-8abx+1=0
\end{align*}
From this, we can use Vieta's formulas to compare the coefficients. We will use the fact that 
\begin{align*}
    \alpha+\beta&=\frac{-b}{a}\\
    \alpha\beta&=\frac{c}{a}
\end{align*}
Applying this to our two quadratics gives that 
\begin{align*}
    \alpha+\beta=\frac{-4b}{a-b}=\frac{8ab}{4ab}=2
\end{align*}
Which simplifies to 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{-4b}{a-b}&=2\\
    -4b&=2a-2b\\\
    b&=-a
\end{align*}
Using a similar technique we get that
\begin{align*}
    \alpha\beta=\frac{4b+c}{b-a}&=\frac{1}{4ab}\\
    4ab(4b+c)&=b-a
\end{align*}
But from the previous result $b=-a$, and so this expression can be simplified to get $c=\frac{1+8a^2}{2a}$. Which is the same value that we obtained using the alternate methods.


\section{Generalisations and Related Problems}

Within our Tripos Problem, there were multiple conditions that had to be satisfied: the two curves are parabolas, they are a distance of two apart, and they intersect each other at right angles. 
In order to generalise the problem, we could broaden any of these conditions, and consequently think about how our original method could be applied to a more general problem.

Whilst changing distance between them is not very helpful in finding a generalisation, we could adapt the idea of having two parabolas intersect each other to having other graphs intersect each other, such as conics.

\subsection{One-parameter Families of Curves}

\noindent We first need to introduce some new notation. 
Let 
$$X=\{C_c:F(x,y,c)=0\}$$
be a one-parameter family of smooth curves, where c is the parameter. Equating the function $F$ to 0 gives an equation in terms of variables $x$ and $y$, and one parameter $c$ \citep{libretexts}. When $c$ varies, you obtain an entire family of curves.

\noindent In order to understand this concept better, we will take a simple example.
\noindent Let

$$F(x,y,c)=y-(x-c)^2.$$
Then $F(x,y,c)=0$ gives 
$$y=(x-c)^2,$$
which is a parabola with vertex at $(c,0)$. Letting $c$ vary translates the parabola parallel to the x-axis, and so $y=(x-c)^2$ is a one-parameter family of  parabolic curves.

An orthogonal trajectory of X is a curve that meets every member of X at right angles. This is very useful in relation to our problem, as the two parabolas having to intersect at right angles is precisely the orthogonal-trajectories concept. We are seeking two parabolas (either from the same family or two different families) that meet orthogonally.

\subsection{Generalising the Types of Graphs}

\noindent Using the concepts of one-parameter families and orthogonal trajectories, we are now able to find a curve that intersects a whole family of parabolas at right angles. 

\noindent Let us define $$F(x,y,a)=y-ax^2.$$
Setting to $F(x,y,a)=0$, we obtain a family of parabolas of the form $$y=ax^2.$$

\noindent We can now employ a similar method to what we used to solve our original problem. 

\noindent Differentiating our equation for the family of curves gives 
$$y'=2ax.$$
Substituting in $a=\frac{y}{x^2}$, we obtain

\begin{align*}
   y'&=2(\frac{y}{x^2})x\\
   &=\frac{2y}{x}.
\end{align*}

\noindent As the curve we are trying to find intersects every member of the family orthogonally, the derivative of this curve must be the negative reciprocal of the derivative of the family of curves. Hence,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{1}{y'}\\
    &=-\frac{x}{2y}.
\end{align*}

\noindent Rearranging this gives us the differential equation
$$2y\;dy=-x\;dx.$$

\noindent Integrating both sides, we obtain 
$$y^2=-\frac{x^2}{2}+c.$$

\noindent This is the equation of an ellipse, centred at $(0,0)$. Therefore, the orthogonal trajectory of the family of parabolas $y=ax^2$ is the family of ellipses of the form $y^2=-\frac{x^2}{2}+c$.

